mode: "100644"
text: "# The Voice Analysis Framework\n\nThis is how you reverse-engineer a voice from writing — the difference between this skill and\nevery \"describe your brand voice\" tool. Work the six layers in order. For each, you're doing\nthree things: **observing** specific features, **citing** the evidence (quote the sample), and\n**translating** the observation into a reproducible rule.\n\n> Rule of the whole framework: *no observation without evidence.* If you can't point to a line\n> in a sample, you're guessing — and guesses are what make AI content feel generic.\n\nA fast way to surface patterns: read 2–3 samples and underline anything you could imagine the\nwriter doing *again*. Recurring choices are voice; one-offs are noise.\n\n---\n\n## Layer 1 — Lexicon (the words)\n\nWhat words they reach for is the most immediate voice signal.\n\n**Observe:**\n- **Register** — Anglo-Saxon and plain (\"use,\" \"buy,\" \"help\") vs Latinate and formal\n  (\"utilize,\" \"purchase,\" \"facilitate\"). Most distinctive voices skew plain.\n- **Contractions** — \"don't / you're / it's\" vs \"do not / you are / it is.\" A single biggest\n  formality tell. Note their default and any exceptions.\n- **Signature words & phrases** — words they reach for repeatedly; their name for the product,\n  the customer, the category (\"members\" vs \"users\"; \"ship\" vs \"launch\"). Pet phrases.\n- **Concreteness** — abstract (\"solutions,\" \"value,\" \"experiences\") vs concrete and sensory\n  (\"a spreadsheet that emails you at 2am\"). Concrete is usually the better voice.\n- **Intensifiers & hedges** — \"very, really, honestly, literally\" vs \"kind of, maybe, I think,\n  sort of.\" The ratio reveals confidence.\n- **Jargon level** — how much insider language, and whether it's used to include or to flex.\n- **Slang / profanity** — present? what kind? how strong? This is a hard guardrail to capture.\n\n**Translate to a rule:** \"Plain Anglo-Saxon vocabulary; always contracts; calls them\n'builders' not 'users'; bans 'solution,' 'leverage,' 'utilize'; one concrete example per post.\"\n\n---\n\n## Layer 2 — Syntax (how sentences are built)\n\nRhythm is voice you can feel before you can name. It lives in sentence construction.\n\n**Observe:**\n- **Sentence length & variance** — uniform medium sentences read as corporate. Distinctive\n  voices vary: a string of short punches, then one long flowing sentence. Measure roughly\n  (short ≈ <10 words, long ≈ >25) and note the *pattern*, not just the average.\n- **Openers** — do sentences/posts start with \"I,\" with \"And/But,\" with a question, with a\n  number, with the subject? Recurring openers are a strong signature.\n- **Fragments** — do they use them on purpose? \"No fluff. Just the work.\" Deliberate fragments\n  are a major voice marker.\n- **Active vs passive** — \"we shipped it\" vs \"it was shipped.\" Most strong voices are active.\n- **Parallelism & triads** — do they build in threes? List in rhythm? \"Faster, cheaper,\n  yours.\"\n- **Clause complexity** — simple declaratives vs subordinate-clause-heavy, comma-spliced flow.\n- **Punctuation as rhythm** — em-dashes for interruption, colons for setup-payoff, ellipses\n  for trailing, periods for staccato. Note their default rhythm tools.\n\n**Translate to a rule:** \"Short declaratives, 6–12 words, broken by one long sentence per\nparagraph. Frequent fragments for emphasis. Opens many lines with 'And' or 'But.' Em-dashes,\nnever semicolons.\"\n\n---\n\n## Layer 3 — Rhetoric (the moves)\n\nThe recurring devices that make a point land their way.\n\n**Observe:**\n- **Rhetorical questions** — do they ask to set up an answer? How often?\n- **Direct address** — how much \"you\"? Intimate and conversational, or detached third-person?\n- **Repetition / anaphora** — repeated openings or phrases for rhythm and emphasis.\n- **Analogy & metaphor** — frequency and *type*. Sports? cooking? engineering? pop culture? The\n  metaphor domain is itself a fingerprint.\n- **Contrast / antithesis** — \"not X, but Y.\" \"Most people Z. The best people W.\" A workhorse\n  of opinionated content; note if they lean on it.\n- **Assertion vs story** — do they state a claim, or tell a small story to imply it?\n- **Humor** — type (dry, self-deprecating, absurd, deadpan, none) and target (themselves, the\n  category, never the customer). Capture the *kind*, and the lines it won't cross.\n- **The turn** — how they pivot or land the point: a callback, a one-line mic-drop, a question,\n  a reframe. Most strong writers have a signature landing.\n\n**Translate to a rule:** \"Self-deprecating dry humor, never at the customer's expense. Builds\narguments by contrast ('everyone does X; do Y instead'). Lands posts with a short reframe, not\na CTA.\"\n\n---\n\n## Layer 4 — Structure (how a piece is organized)\n\nZoom out from the sentence to the whole post.\n\n**Observe:**\n- **Opening move** — hook style: provocation, question, stat, confession, in-media-res story.\n- **Build** — linear argument, layered (claim → evidence → implication), listicle, or narrative.\n- **Close** — CTA, callback to the open, mic-drop line, soft fade, question. Note the default.\n- **Paragraphing & whitespace** — one-line paragraphs? dense blocks? The visual rhythm on the\n  feed is part of voice.\n- **Formatting habits** — bold, lists, the \"hook → blank line → body\" LinkedIn pattern, emoji\n  as bullets, arrows. Capture the recurring scaffolding.\n\n**Translate to a rule:** \"Opens with a one-line provocation, blank line, then 3–4 short\nparagraphs building by example, closes on a question. Heavy whitespace; no lists.\"\n\n---\n\n## Layer 5 — Stance (relationship and attitude)\n\nThe hardest layer to fake and the easiest to feel. It's the *posture* toward the reader.\n\n**Observe:**\n- **Distance** — intimate (\"between us…\") vs formal (\"one might consider\"). Peer or podium?\n- **Authority posture** — peer (\"I'm figuring this out too\"), mentor (\"here's what I learned\"),\n  expert (\"here's how it works\"), or provocateur (\"you're doing it wrong\").\n- **Confidence** — assertive and unhedged vs careful and qualified.\n- **Emotional register** — warm, cool, intense, playful, earnest. The temperature.\n- **Self-disclosure** — how much do they reveal? Vulnerable, or guarded?\n- **For / against** — what they champion and what they push against. Strong voices have enemies\n  (ideas, not people).\n\n**Translate to a rule:** \"Peer-to-peer, lightly authoritative — 'here's what worked for me,'\nnever 'you must.' Warm but unsentimental. Shares failures openly; keeps private life off-limits.\nAgainst hustle-culture theater.\"\n\n---\n\n## Layer 6 — Negative space (what they never do)\n\nOften the most defining layer, and the one everyone skips. A voice is shaped as much by its\nomissions as its habits.\n\n**Observe what's conspicuously absent:**\n- Punctuation they never use (no exclamation marks? no emoji? no semicolons?).\n- Words/constructions they avoid (no buzzwords? no superlatives? never \"I'm excited to\n  announce\"?).\n- Tones they won't strike (never salesy? never preachy? never cutesy?).\n- Moves they won't make (never humble-brags? never dunks on people? never uses hashtags?).\n\n**Translate to hard \"never\"s:** these become guardrails every downstream skill must respect.\n\"Never uses exclamation marks. Never opens with 'I'm thrilled.' Never more than one emoji.\nNever dunks on a named competitor.\"\n\n---\n\n## From layers to fingerprint\n\nAfter working all six layers, you'll have dozens of observations. Distill to the **5–7 most\ndistinctive, reproducible signatures** — the ones that carry the most recognition per word.\nTest each candidate: *if a post broke this rule, would it feel off?* If yes, it's fingerprint\nmaterial. If it's true but generic (\"uses clear language\"), cut it.\n\nA strong fingerprint reads like a recipe a stranger could follow — and like a fingerprint, it\nshould be unique enough to identify the writer in a lineup.\n"
